<PageLayout
  title="Missing Person Case Support in Indiana | Silver Lining of Hope"
  description="Free help for families of missing persons in Indiana and nearby states: case research, records requests, database entries, and public awareness."
  heading="Missing persons"
  intro="More than a thousand people are missing in Indiana alone. Behind every entry is a family that deserves real help."
  crumbs={[
    { label: 'Programs', href: '/programs' },
    { label: 'Missing persons', href: '/programs/missing-persons' },
  ]}
>
  <div class="container--narrow section prose">
    <p>
      The "P" in HOPE stands for persons missing. When someone disappears,
      their family is thrown into a system almost no one understands in
      advance: which agency takes the report, what NamUs is, why a case does
      or doesn't get attention, what records exist and who will release them.
      This program exists to give families of missing persons in Indiana —
      and increasingly in Ohio, Illinois, Michigan, and Kentucky — free,
      organized, persistent support from the first day to the last.
    </p>

    <h2>How we support missing person cases</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Getting the case properly recorded.</strong> We help families
        confirm the police report exists, verify the case is entered into
        NCIC, and create or complete a
        <a href="https://namus.nij.ojp.gov/" rel="noopener">NamUs</a> profile
        with dental, DNA, and identifying information — the single most
        important step for long-term cases.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Cross-referencing databases.</strong> We check and reconcile
        entries across NamUs, the Doe Network, and the Charley Project, and
        we monitor unidentified persons listings for possible matches —
        carefully, and always through proper channels.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Records research.</strong> Using Indiana's APRA and public
        records laws in surrounding states, we request the reports, logs, and
        documents that let a family understand what has actually been done on
        their case.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Timeline building.</strong> We assemble verified movements,
        communications, and sightings into a clean timeline that helps
        investigators and search efforts focus.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Responsible awareness.</strong> We help families create
        accurate flyers and social posts, coordinate with media, and avoid
        the misinformation that can genuinely damage a case.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Long-term and legacy cases</h2>
    <p>
      Missing person cases don't expire, and neither does our help. Some of
      the families we serve have been searching for decades. For those cases,
      records research and database completeness matter most — which is why
      this pillar feeds directly into
      <a href="/programs/the-missing-link">The Missing Link</a>, our project
      to compile and cross-reference every available record on Indiana's
      1,000+ missing persons into one public, searchable directory, mapped by
      Indiana State Police district.
    </p>

    <h2>If your loved one is missing right now</h2>
    <p>
      Don't wait to report — there is no waiting period to report a missing
      adult or child in Indiana. Call 911 or your local agency, then read
      <a href="/get-help/families">what to do when a loved one goes
      missing</a> for a step-by-step guide. When you're ready,
      <a href="/about/contact">contact us</a> and we'll take as much of the
      research burden off your shoulders as we can. If you have information
      about someone else's disappearance, you can
      <a href="/get-help/submit-a-tip">submit a tip anonymously</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</PageLayout>
